nodecode-sluice is a rewrite of orjinameh's orjinameh/airlock:

  https://github.com/orjinameh/airlock
  commit e0e5084dcf559c5a392549aa1a892bcf75b64154
  MIT License, Copyright (c) 2026 Peter Stephen (LICENSE)

Rewritten: the store, the review and the apply, in Common Lisp. A workspace
is copied twice under <NODECODE_HOME>/plugin-data/sluice/ -- the stage the
agent works in and the baseline it is compared to -- with .gitignore rules
and dependency folders kept out, symlinks that point outside the workspace
listed as skipped, and files over the section's size limit skipped by name.
The manifest of digests, the staged copy the review reads, the numbered
units (a block of changed lines, or a created, deleted, renamed, replaced or
mode-changed file), the atomic per-file apply with its conflict and
symlink-escape checks, the stage discarded when asked, and stages untouched
for 14 days pruned, are the plugin's, module for module: stage, diffing,
applying, classify. So is the irreversibility classifier: an unquoted ; | &
or newline separates commands, a NAME=value prefix and sudo are read
through, bash -c and eval are read recursively, and installs, pushes,
network writes, publishes, outside writes and deletes, and a downloaded
script run by a shell are the findings it names. The primer, the four tools
and /sluice carry the plugin's own words where they still apply.

Its shape is four agent-facing tools -- sluice_run, sluice_diff, sluice_apply,
sluice_discard -- and one command, /sluice, instead of Hermes's terminal
environment provider.

Left out, and why: Hermes's terminal-environment seam (the provider class,
its probe, its prompt note and the path rewriting applied to every tool call
the harness made) is not rewritten, because Nodecode's shell is its own and
nothing here can put a copy under it; the rewriting survives inside
sluice_run, which maps the workspace's paths into the stage and back. The
pre_tool_call hook and its approval prompt are replaced by the gate in
sluice_run: a held command answers NOT CONTAINED and runs nothing until the
call says allow: true, where the plugin raised Hermes's own prompt (and
failed closed in unattended runs). The plugin's SHA-256 digests are kept,
a change detector rather than a signature; its fcntl file lock between two
applies is not carried over, nor is rename detection across a directory
move; its pytest suite, config_schema and doctor output are not either: the
section declaration and this folder's tests are their counterparts.
